      <li><strong>Flat socio-economic gradient:</strong> Income, parent education, internet, and tuition all show negligible effect sizes on HSC results in this sample. The policy lever is not simply "more resources" but "better use of resources."</li>
      <li><strong>Zero attendance-performance link:</strong> The correlation of {s['att_corr']:.3f} challenges the assumption that higher attendance automatically drives better outcomes. Quality of instruction and engagement matter more than seat time.</li>
      <li><strong>Stagnant progression:</strong> The GPA delta of {s['delta_mean']:+.3f} indicates the system is not accelerating learning between prior assessments and HSC. In some districts, students regress.</li>
      <li><strong>Modest regional inequality:</strong> Districts differ by ~0.3 GPA points. Rangpur and Rajshahi need targeted support, but gaps are not extreme.</li>
      <li><strong>No at-risk tail:</strong> With 0% below 3.0, the priority is uplifting Average performers (GPA 3.0–3.99) into Good/Excellent bands.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
    <div class="success-box">
      <strong>1. Shift from attendance tracking to engagement metrics.</strong> Pilot classroom-participation indices, formative assessment completion rates, and teacher-student interaction quality scores in under-performing districts.
    </div>
    <div class="success-box">
      <strong>2. Target district-level instructional quality, not just infrastructure.</strong> Rangpur and Rajshahi show negative progression deltas. Deploy subject-matter coaching for teachers and standardized diagnostic tests to identify curriculum gaps.
    </div>
    <div class="success-box">
      <strong>3. Re-examine private tuition and internet programs.</strong> These resources show minimal population-level impact. Redirect funds toward structured digital content (e.g., MOOCs aligned with HSC syllabus) and supervised study groups rather than blanket access or coaching subsidies.
    </div>
    <div class="success-box">
      <strong>4. Introduce early-warning systems for progression decline.</strong> Use Previous GPA and SSC results to flag students at risk of negative deltas. Interventions (mentoring, peer tutoring) should trigger before HSC, not after.
    </div>
    <div class="success-box">
      <strong>5. Expand the income range in future data collection.</strong> The current sample (35k–60k BDT/month) may miss the true effect of poverty on academic outcomes. Policymakers need data from rural and ultra-low-income households to design equity-focused funding.
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    Report generated from <code>data.csv</code> (n = 1,000) | All statistics computed directly from the dataset | No values fabricated.
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
"""

